---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ContactInvitation from '../components/ContactInvitation.astro';
import EditorialModel from '../components/EditorialModel.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import PartnershipBrief from '../components/PartnershipBrief.astro';
---
<Layout title='Partnerships' description='Bring a different perspective to NRTH. Discuss operational, technical, research, material or experience partnerships.'>
 <section class='page-heading'><span class='section-label'>Partnerships</span><h1>Bring something<br/><em>we don’t see yet.</em></h1><p>Different expertise can change the question. Bring the work you understand, the knowledge you can contribute and the possibility you want to explore with NRTH.</p><a class='inline-link' href='#prepare'>Shape your introduction</a></section>
 <EditorialModel kind='partnership' dark/>
 <section class='editorial-split'><div data-enter><span class='section-label'>Potential starting points</span><h2>The connection<br/>is the beginning.</h2><p class='editorial-route-reference'>These are examples of useful opening questions. The fit, scope and expectations of a collaboration are discussed for the actual opportunity.</p></div><div class='text-stack' data-enter><article><h3>Work you understand deeply.</h3><p>Bring an operational challenge, a decision or a handoff. Describe what happens today, who is involved and the exception that exposes the problem.</p><p>A transport coordinator could examine how a changed assignment reaches the next team. A care team could explore which information people need at a particular handoff. Start with the work before deciding what the answer should look like.</p><a class='inline-link' href='/industries/mobility'>Explore the mobility context</a></article><article><h3>Knowledge worth investigating.</h3><p>Bring a method, a technical question or specialist expertise. Explain what you know, the evidence supporting it and the uncertainty that a focused investigation might resolve.</p><p>Material expertise could inform an application requirement. Software expertise could clarify an information boundary. Research could give an early assumption a practical test.</p><a class='inline-link' href='/research'>Prepare an experiment outline</a></article><article><h3>A reason to gather.</h3><p>Begin with the people and the purpose of the moment. Share the experience you want to create, the setting and the production conditions around it.</p><p>A useful introduction connects creative intention to practical details: audience, format, timing, accessibility and the contributions that the experience needs.</p><a class='inline-link' href='/industries/events'>Explore the events context</a></article></div></section>
 <section class='editorial-split section-dark'><div data-enter><span class='section-label'>Make the introduction useful</span><h2>A clear question.<br/>An honest starting point.</h2></div><div class='disclosure-list' data-enter><details open><summary>What should the introduction include?<Icon name='plus'/></summary><p>Describe the opportunity in a few sentences, the people it concerns, your contribution and any important timing or constraints. Point to the relevant NRTH venture if one is clear; an idea can also begin across the portfolio.</p></details><details><summary>What if the direction is still early?<Icon name='plus'/></summary><p>Say which part is uncertain. A question, a sketch or an anonymized example can establish a useful context. Avoid presenting an exploratory concept as a finished capability or an agreed engagement.</p></details><details><summary>How are responsibilities established?<Icon name='plus'/></summary><p>A discussion about the actual opportunity defines the proposed work, participants, information needs and responsibilities. Confidentiality, intellectual property, timing and commercial arrangements require their own agreement where relevant.</p></details><details><summary>What happens to the outline below?<Icon name='plus'/></summary><p>The builder prepares an introduction that you can copy, download or carry into the contact form. Continuing lets you review and edit the message with your contact details before choosing to share it.</p></details></div></section>
 <PartnershipBrief/>
 <ContactInvitation project='partnership' headline='Bring your perspective.'/>
</Layout>
